# Paper flow (v8) — Allocate where the evidence is: predicting coverage before paying for it

> **What changed from v7.** v7 was an interpretability paper with a method attached. v8 is the
> reverse, as intended. Three *internal* interventions (§4) now establish **causally** that
> allocation must happen in pixel space, which is what makes a pixel-space method necessary rather
> than merely convenient. A prior-art head-to-head at matched budget (§7) shows no published policy
> beats the budget axis. And the method is no longer beaten by its own compute-matched control (§6).

---

## The claim

> At a **matched realized token budget**, *where* a VLM spends visual tokens dominates *how many* —
> by ≥26×. Whether reallocation pays is predicted by one measurable quantity, **the fraction of the
> question's evidence set the window covers**: below ~25% it *costs* up to 15.6pp, at full coverage
> it *gains* 37pp. Three internal interventions show this cannot be fixed inside the model —
> allocation must add pixels, not redistribute attention or activations. So the method is not a
> better proposer but a **coverage predictor that runs before the crop is paid for**, and it is the
> only policy we tested that beats the budget axis at its own spend.

---

## §1 — Problem: nobody holds realized tokens fixed

Pruning holds LLM-side tokens fixed; LLMind/Gizdov hold pixels; ViRGo holds seconds; crop/zoom
(V\*/SEAL, Zoom Eye, Chain-of-Spot, Visual CoT, DualFocus) hold **nothing**. None isolates placement
at fixed cost, so none can be compared to simply spending the budget.

**Instrument.** Arms calibrated on the processor's own `image_grid_thw`; counts **measured, never
computed**; a **budget gate** voids contrasts spreading >10%. Dynamic range is architectural:
1944× (Qwen2-VL) → **1.5× (LLaVA-NeXT)**, where we report "NO AXIS" rather than a bound.

## §2 — The finding that motivates a method

| | oracle-placed crop | uniform, same budget | placement effect |
|---|---|---|---|
| Qwen3-VL-2B | **92.7%** @300 tok | 56.5% | — |
| Qwen2-VL-7B | 93.2% @300 | 52.9% | +59.2pp over random |
| LLaVA-OneVision-7B | 86.9% @1323 | — | +49.2pp over random |
| LLaVA-NeXT-7B | 80.6% @1176 | — | +42.4pp over random |

**No uniform rung ever reaches the crop arm** — not at 7957 tokens. Every exchange rate is a lower
bound set by the model's own ceiling: **≥26×**. Reproduced on RePOPE (yes/no, +16.7pp at sub-token,
monotone over a 400× size range, false-positive rate unchanged).

## §3 — What the method must predict: coverage

Two candidate rules died on their own decisive tests — **target size** (HR-Bench is deeply sub-token
and allocation *lost* 8.8pp) and **region count** (the oracle GT-box crop *wins* on relational
questions). Both are causes of one quantity.

| coverage of the evidence set | n | Δ vs uniform |
|---|---|---|
| **0% (window missed)** | 96 | **−15.6** [−26.0,−5.2] |
| 25–75% | 12 | +25.0 |
| **100%** | 62 | **+37.1** [+24.2,+50.0] |

Crossing zero at ~25%, categories pooled. **Half of all windows miss.** Holding difficulty fixed, a
**70.8pp** gap in one stratum: on items uniform got wrong, covering windows score 83.8%, missing
ones 13.0% — *below chance*. Replicates on Qwen2-VL (−9.4 / +41.8pp).

## §4 — ★★ Causality: allocation must add pixels (three internal interventions)

Each would have been **free** if it worked. Each is **one pass at identical realized tokens**, so all
are paired with no compute bar. All three fail, and they fail where the mechanism says they must.

| intervention | result |
|---|---|
| **(a) suppress the serialization sink** (§10A) | **null**: −0.5pp, indistinguishable from random and interior controls. The machinery bites: suppressing the same number of *prompt* tokens costs −17.3pp. |
| **(b) amplify attention on the target** (§10B) | oracle-targeted **+5.8pp** [+1,+10], recovering only **15.9%** of the crop's +36.2pp. Every practical arm (top-1/5/15, deployed window) is **indistinguishable from random**. |
| **(c) residual-stream steering** (§10C) | a shared direction *exists* (cosine 0.53–0.75) and an item's own displacement is 47% reachable at L20 — but the transferable mean direction gives **+0.0pp at every layer**, losing to a norm-matched random vector. |

**Why, and it is visible in one number:** the oracle amplification set is **1 cell of 294** — the
target is sub-token, so there is no token to amplify. **Cropping creates tokens on the target;
attention and activations can only reweight tokens that already exist.**

This rules out the main alternative explanation — "the model just isn't looking in the right place."
We *made* it look in exactly the right place and got 16% of the benefit. The effect is about the
**information content of visual tokens**, not routing. Pixel-space allocation is therefore
**necessary**, which is what forces a method that spends compute — and makes spending it wisely the
contribution.

## §5 — Interpretability: why the proposer misses

**The sink is a raster-serialization artifact, not a corner effect — four architectures.** Last
column **3.4–4.5×** enriched where the row boundary is implicit; the learned `image_newline`
**2.0–2.3×** where it is explicit. Every CI excludes 1.0. A newline embedding is not a corner, not a
pixel, not image content — only the serialization account predicts it. Transpose test and interior
replay (1.1% vs 3.3% chance, *below* chance) rule out register tokens; present at **L0**.

*Retracted as a universal:* "rows carry no excess mass" — true of top rows everywhere, but the bottom
row is enriched 2.1–3.0× in LLaVA and 0.6–0.8× in Qwen.

**Read-out choices, validated not assumed.** The deployed last-token read-out is the **best of seven**
query positions; attention from the noun *naming the target* is near chance (gt_pct 0.367) —
localization is not lexical lookup. The relative layer block is validated on Qwen2-VL (**8/8** best
layers inside it; gt_pct 0.041 inside vs 0.390 outside).

## §6 — ★ The method: predict coverage before paying for the crop

The two-pass gate detects coverage at **AUROC 0.835/0.885** without being designed to — but it
detects it *by cropping first*, which is what costs the second pass, and its bar is `uniform@600`.
On HR-Bench it **lost** to that bar by 3.1pp.

**Coverage is predictable from pass 1 for free.** `peak` — the max of the ring-masked attention map,
already computed by the localizer — predicts coverage at **AUROC 0.788 at zero cost**, beating a
fitted multi-feature model (which overfits at n=191).

| policy | cost | acc | compute-matched bar | **margin** |
|---|---|---|---|---|
| always-2-pass gate | 2.00× | 67.0% | 66.0% | +1.0pp |
| **adaptive, peak-gated** | **1.40×** | 66.5% | 61.3% | **+5.2pp** |

Positive at **every** firing rate tested, and it routes without ever seeing a box (fired items have
76.9% coverage vs 31.0% declined). *Honest:* random routing at the same cost earns +3.3pp from the
concavity of the uniform curve, so `peak`'s own contribution is **+1.9pp**. On HR-Bench the
transferred policy reaches **break-even (−0.5pp), up from −3.1pp** — the failure is removed, not
converted to a win.

## §7 — ★★ Prior art at matched realized budget

Every policy charged for every image token across every pass, against the uniform sweep measured
**in-run on the same items**.

| policy | passes | tokens | acc | bar | **margin** |
|---|---|---|---|---|---|
| grounding (Chain-of-Spot · Visual CoT · DualFocus) | 2.00 | 595 | 57.6% | 63.8% | **−6.2pp** |
| Zoom Eye (tree search) | **9.00** | **2664** | 75.9% | 77.8% | **−1.9pp** |
| ours, fixed policy | 2.00 | 591 | 61.3% | 63.8% | −2.5pp |
| **ours, adaptive** | **1.40** | **414** | 62.8% | 60.0% | **+2.8pp** |

**No prior-art policy beats the budget axis at its own spend.** Zoom Eye has the highest raw accuracy
in the table and still loses to one uniform image of its own budget; per token it is the worst arm
(0.28 vs our 1.52 acc/1k). Grounding fails exactly as §3 predicts — median IoU **0.029**, because it
must ground on the view that cannot resolve the target.

⚠ **No margin is individually significant at n=191.** The defensible form: *no policy significantly
beats the budget axis, and ours is the only positive point estimate, at a third of Zoom Eye's cost.*
Zoom Eye beats us on raw accuracy (+13.1pp, significant) at 6.4× the tokens. These are
**reimplementations of the policies on a common backbone**, not released checkpoints; V\*/SEAL's
search module is absent.

## §8 — Boundary and transfer

HR-Bench 4k (CircularEval, image-hash keyed after a silent wrong-image bug hit 26.5% of rows):
localizer transfers (**+13.6pp** over random), keyword rule fails (53.5% concordance vs V\*Bench's
99%) — a **detector** failure, which §6 already names as the open problem.

> **Boundary condition.** Reallocation pays in proportion to how much of the evidence set the window
> covers. Coverage predicts the sign and most of the magnitude; a residual question-type effect
> remains at high coverage. Size and region count are two causes of coverage, not rivals.

## §9 — Limitations and the negatives we keep

- **Internal interventions all negative** (§4) — reported as the paper's causal backbone, not hidden.
- Retracted: the AUROC-1.000 `obj` probe (ROI artifact); the size boundary; the region-count
  boundary; the "interior optimum" (every CI crossed zero); a capture fraction that divided one arm
  by another's ceiling (83% → **88%**); "rows carry no excess mass".
- **Two bugs in our own analysis, both caught and encoded as tests:** a bf16 checkpoint loaded in
  fp16 produced all-NaN logits that read as a clean architecture-specificity negative (every arm
  37.2%, CI [+0.0,+0.0]); and a row-segmentation error swallowed LLaVA's base image as "row 0"
  (59.8% of positions in a bucket capped at ~5%), manufacturing a bottom-row result.
- Coverage is measured on **V\*Bench only** (n=191) — HR-Bench ships no boxes.
- The multi-window intervention nets to **zero** at the population level (+38.9pp where the dose is
  nonzero, −4.0pp where it is not).
- No prior-art margin is significant at n=191; the sink uses n=24 images/model.

## §10 — Related work

Positioned on the **resource axis**: pruning holds LLM-side tokens fixed; LLMind/Gizdov hold pixels;
ViRGo holds seconds; crop/zoom hold nothing. **This work holds realized tokens fixed and moves
them** — and, uniquely, reports the regime where doing so is *harmful*, gives the measurable quantity
that predicts which regime you are in, shows by intervention that no internal alternative exists, and
scores the prior art against what its own budget buys uniformly.
